But you can't really say that you're doing a data quality assessment of that file because in order to do that, you really need to know what were the requirements, right? So many of you found some anomalies. For example, one that

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:18.000
That I like, well, first of all, you found a lot of missing data, right? So, but the question is, is that a data quality

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.000
problem or issue

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:35.000
If the requirements for that column don't require it to be complete, they don't require a value to be there, it's an optional value. In that case, a missing value is not a data quality problem, because it's

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:47.000
Violating a data quality requirement or a data requirement. So again, you can't really do a data quality assessment of data

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:52.000
Until you know what the data requirements are. So in this new

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:59.000
Assignment is sort of halfway between analysis and data quality assessment because

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:16.000
Again, we're approaching this from the standpoint that we need to start with the product that we're building. What is the information product that we're building? So what I tried to describe in the new assignment is I want some kind of a product. I want a report

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:22.000
And here's what I want in the report. So here are the requirements for the report

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:30.000
But I'm not giving you the report, I'm giving you some data that I want you to use to make this report. And based on that

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:46.000
You need to go back and say, well, if I need this in the report, what do I need in the data that comes in, and what do I need… what requirements do I need for it? Is this something that's going to be required? In which case, I need a data requirement to say

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:58.000
that this particular item needs to be there. It needs to be filled in so that I have it for the report because that's a requirement in the report.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.000
So again, it's starting with the end in mind

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:08.000
You can't really do a good data quality assessment or even create data quality requirements

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:20.000
Until you know what it is that you're trying to build. So again, this is a really important part of this course. And so there's no like fixed answers

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:36.000
It's sort of like when you're learning mathematics or geometry, it's not so important that you remember all of the theorems or all the types of calculations. It's how… but it's more important what you think about, how is your thinking, what's your logic

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:46.000
that you're going through. And the same thing is true here. I want you, in this course, to think of yourself as the information quality analyst

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:59.000
And you want to analyze things, right? So in some cases, it may be very obvious what you need in the data, or it's very obvious that some of the data is missing, or it's not right or correct

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:14.000
But in many cases, you just don't know, you're not sure, and so you need to say, well, my analysis is I need to follow up and there needs to be more work done here to understand exactly what was meant or what was needed

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:26.000
So, again, think of yourselves as analysts. Don't be afraid to say, well, I'm not sure about this because it's not clear from the specifications for the product

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:36.000
exactly what is needed here, and so there needs to be more investigation, or I need to go get more data, or I need to get something else that I don't already have

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:42.000
So think of it in that way as being an analyst, not just

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:50.000
Not all the answers are plain and clear. They require some work

00:09:50.000 --> 00:10:00.000
So again, this gets to this point that I'm trying to make is that when we talked about the seven basic principles of information quality

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.000
We said that one of them, and it may be really the most important one

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:09.000
is that you need to manage information as product

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:17.000
If you're thinking about information and what information you need about the quality of the information, the data quality

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:24.000
You really need to anchor that in understanding of what it is that I'm trying to build

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:30.000
Again, going back to the manufacturing analogy, I'm trying to build something

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.000
If it's an information product or service

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:47.000
then it's something that's based on data. And so how do I go about managing that and analyzing that? And the way that you want to start is you want to start with understanding

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:51.000
What I'm trying to build

00:10:51.000 --> 00:11:01.000
So again, you can think of your information systems in your organization as these information product factories. And again, we go back to the same analogy

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:08.000
And that is, we have raw materials, that's our source data, and it could be multiple sources of data. Typically, it is

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:17.000
We put that data into our machinery, which in our case is mostly programs, these operational systems that we run

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:22.000
And typically now, everybody has a data warehouse where we not only

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:38.000
generating the information products that we need to run the business or the organization from day to day, but we save all that in a data warehouse that we can analyze later to see what we call business intelligence

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:41.000
To see how we can do things better

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:46.000
And this is the whole idea of a data warehouse, and almost everybody has one

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:57.000
And then we have a design blueprint for what we're building. We're building an airplane or a car or toaster, whatever it is. We have design specifications

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:01.000
They're typically not blueprints on paper anymore. They're CAD/CAMs digitized

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:08.000
But nevertheless, we have some design and some requirements for what we're building

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:23.000
And we build those information products, and I call them products, but they could be services too, right? Now more and more nowadays, we're building information services that people ask and go into sort of like these

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:31.000
large language models, Gemini and ChatGPT, you can think of those as information services, right?

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:41.000
You're putting information in, you're getting information back. So it's more of a service than just a fixed report. You get a report out of it, you get a response

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:46.000
But the way they operate is more as a service than as a single fixed product.

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:49.000
But nevertheless, whether it's product or service

00:12:49.000 --> 00:13:07.000
We're building it for somebody to use, right? If you're the user of your chat GPT or you're here in school and you need a copy of your transcript or your diploma, or you need to know your class schedule, those are all information products that are being produced

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:13.000
Out of our information systems, which by the way, constantly keeps changing

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:19.000
We used to be DuryWorks, and now we're using Student Workday, and before that we used

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:21.000
The Boss system

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:25.000
And so anyway, it's a moving target. But nevertheless

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:32.000
The whole idea is that we want to start right here at the beginning. We want to start here

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:45.000
With what it is that we're building and who we're building it for, because that's what shapes the requirements for the product. If we're trying to help somebody, some consumer of this product or service.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:14:02.000
We need to understand what do they need, and that then becomes a requirement for the product. Once we know the requirement for the product, then we can go back up and say, what are the requirements for the data that we need? What data do we need, and what is the condition of that data

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:10.000
So that we can put it through this operational system and build this information product or service that the consumer wants.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:13.000
So again, we want to work backwards

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:22.000
Really, actually, from the consumer to the product, back through how we want our operational systems to work and what

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:25.000
what data that they need to work properly

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:42.000
So again, so much of our thinking around information quality and data quality, especially, and I go into a lot of companies and they ask me about it and I look at their systems and what they're doing. They're only focused on the left hand side of this

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:54.000
They're just saying, I'm getting data in, I'm going to clean it up really clean, and this is mostly what, when you were doing your analysis, right? You were just looking at some piece of data

00:14:54.000 --> 00:15:09.000
In that case, it was a list of movies, but it really depends what the quality of that. Even though many of you said, well, you know, there's a lot of missing values, obviously there was, there's a lot of inconsistency, there were many things that you observe

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:14.000
But you can't really say whether those are good or bad

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:22.000
Until you know how it's going to be used. As I mentioned, you know, there may be a lot of missing values for

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:40.000
For example, for the duration of the video or movie, whatever it was, but if you're building a product for somebody that wants to use this and they don't care about it, that it's not what they want to know, they really want to know

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:42.000
You know, who are the

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:48.000
producers, who were the actors? Who were the

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:55.000
Who was the director, who was the producer? Many things like that. Maybe that's what they're interested in

00:15:55.000 --> 00:16:09.000
That data may or may not be sufficient, but it doesn't require… it's not putting any requirements on the duration. And so to say there's a lot of missing durations is not necessarily a data quality issue

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:24.000
It's not a data quality problem. If it's not going to be used in the final product. So again, you can't really do data quality assessment until you have the requirements for the data, and those data requirements should come from

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:33.000
The consumer requirements for the product or service that you're building. So you need to work backwards in this diagram.

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:35.000
So

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:52.000
So again, what we learned from manufacturing, this is the reason that the Asian companies, Toyota and the Japanese, Korean have become so successful is they adopted these principles of total quality management and there's four basic axioms of it

00:16:52.000 --> 00:17:01.000
Starts off… you have to understand the consumer's needs. If you're going to build something for a consumer, you have to build what they want, right?

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:09.000
So you can't just say, I'm going to build a widget and I'm going to price it at this price, and I hope it sells

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:20.000
If nobody's in the market for widgets, or even if they are in the market for widgets, but they want widgets with certain features and you're not providing those features, you're not going to be successful.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:29.000
So the very first principle is you have to understand what the consumer needs from the thing that you're making

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:37.000
And then once you know what that is, you need a well-defined process to produce the thing that the consumer wants. And that means

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:42.000
As I said, you're going to have to back into the materials that go into it

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:58.000
And it's especially true for an information product. As I said, you know, if the if the consumer is wanting to know certain things about movies, you need to know what those things are. And just because you have a data set that has movie information in

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:03.000
It may not be sufficient or adequate to produce what they want

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:14.000
And you can't really judge how well it's going to… what the quality of it is for that product until you know the product requirements

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:23.000
Then that tells you what the requirements should be for the data, and then you can assess its quality in terms of being an input into that

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:27.000
process for that product.

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:36.000
Another thing you have to understand is the life cycle, and again, as I pointed out, you know, I mean, if you're manufacturing something

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:43.000
The products, and this is true for information products, have a life cycle

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:49.000
People want certain things at certain times, but over time, it changes what it is that they want.

00:18:49.000 --> 00:19:02.000
So you have to understand, is the product that you're building something that you're introducing something early in its life cycle, where people are just now starting to want this

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:15.000
Is it something that's well understood and people, you know that people want it, it's a mature product, or is it something that people really don't need that much anymore

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:31.000
Things have changed, and they don't need this particular information, and they might not need it in a certain timely way. And so you need to constantly think about the things that you're building. What's the demand for them? Where are you in the life cycle of that product

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:36.000
And then another extremely important one, well, these are all four are very important

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:47.000
is that in order to make that bridge between the consumer and all the way back into the materials that need to go into building that product

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:58.000
You need a product manager, because they're the ones who keep on top and understand what it is at this time that your consumers are wanting in these products or services

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:13.000
And as that changes, they can communicate that, translate that back into your organization, to the people that are building these systems to say, all right, here, we need to change. We're getting demand

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:30.000
For this particular feature, here's something that's been missing that people are wanting in this information product. We need to add that in. If we need to add that in, do we already have this information or do we need to go out and obtain other information that we didn't have already?

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:45.000
So that would create new data requirements. We're requiring certain items or data we need to get those and we need to be sure that those items, we have those items, they're correct, they're timely. All of these other issues that we can use to assess

00:20:45.000 --> 00:21:01.000
The data going in. But again, you can't really assess the quality of raw data coming in to an organization until you know exactly how it's going to be used in producing an information product or service

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:06.000
That meets consumer needs

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:16.000
So these are in here. I won't go through this a lot, but if you're doing an analysis, you're going to be a data quality analyst or an information quality analyst

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:27.000
Then again, if you're somebody calls you in, you get a job, and then the first thing you really should be inquiring about is not what data they're getting

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:34.000
But what they're trying to produce, what are the products that they're trying to

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:47.000
produce, and do they really know what these products are? Are they well-defined? Do we know exactly what the product requirements are? And which ones are really important? They may be manufacturing

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:59.000
are producing, you know hundreds of information products, a large corporation, a bank, something like that, but you really need to know where to start. What are the key ones? What's the most important

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:08.000
And who you have to understand, again, based on those principles, who the users are and how they're using those products.

00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:26.000
So this is where there are many methodologies and recommendations for how to manage data quality. And later we'll go in to talk about some of the others. There's the DMBOK, there's the Six Sigma

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:31.000
There's DCAM, there's many other

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:39.000
framework, if you will, that talk about managing data quality, but they all kind of have a different slant to them

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:48.000
And that's true here, but at least we now have a standard, an international standard. It's called the Data Quality Management Reference Model

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:58.000
that doesn't tell you exactly how to do it, but it tells you what are the things that you should be doing in terms of

00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:02.000
the results that you want to get. So we'll talk about this in a minute

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:13.000
So it has three parts to it. It has an implementation process where you're actually doing the data quality, but then there are data-related support processes

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:17.000
And then there's resource provisioning for the processes.

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:19.000
And so

00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:22.000
Let's talk a little bit about each of these

00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:35.000
So implementation. You remember we talked about Schuhart and Deming, and you remember they really laid the foundation for what we mean by data quality.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:40.000
And how to manage it. And they

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:46.000
Schuhart came up with this idea of a cycle, a repeated cycle that starts

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:48.000
with planning

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:53.000
doing what you plan, checking what you did

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:59.000
And then analyzing that to see if it could be improved. Plan, do, check, act

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:03.000
cycle that they called it.

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:11.000
Shuhart actually came up with that, and Deming wanted to kind of rename it as plan do study. He wanted to study

00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:13.000
And then act or analyze

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:16.000
And so

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:22.000
So, one of the great things about the ISO standard is that it's based on the same principle

00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:26.000
It's based on the Schuhart-Deming plan do check act cycle

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:30.000
And so

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:34.000
The data quality planning and requirements is the plan

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:38.000
Data quality control is the

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:46.000
And data quality assurance is the check, and data quality analysis and improvement is the act. So

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:57.000
What we mean by this, and I mentioned this earlier, and again, I want you to come away from this course having a good understanding of the difference between data quality control

00:24:57.000 --> 00:25:00.000
and data quality assurance

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:12.000
So data quality control is what we just got through talking about is where we're building a data or an information product or service. So you're building and you have a product

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:23.000
Data quality control is looking to be sure that the product that you build meets the requirements, that the consumer requirements that you gathered for that product

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:34.000
So data quality control is applied at the end of the process when you've manufactured or built the product that you're trying to build

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:40.000
So data quality control is, does the product, the finished product, meet requirements

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:44.000
So that's its quality. Is it meeting the product requirements?

00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:47.000
Data quality assurance

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:56.000
are the checks that you do during the process, not after you've built the product, but while you're building that product. Like

00:25:56.000 --> 00:26:07.000
The data that comes into the door, you're looking at, like you were doing with the analysis that you just got through doing on the movie file. If that's an input coming into some product

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:24.000
Then data quality assurance is saying, well, I have now certain requirements for that data that's coming in that I'm going to use to build this product. Is it meeting those requirements? Because if the first data coming in the door is not meeting the requirements, then I don't have any hope

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:35.000
That the product that I'm going to build will meet its requirements. It will pass quality control. So data quality assurance are things that you do to assure

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:39.000
that data quality control will succeed.

00:26:39.000 --> 00:26:44.000
Control happens at the product, assurance happens during the process

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:57.000
Many people, even many people who are working supposedly in quality roles don't clearly understand the difference between data quality control and data quality

00:26:57.000 --> 00:27:12.000
even if they're not doing data, if they're doing, for example, software, or they're manufacturing, quality control is when you're building quality control is making sure that

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:17.000
The finished product meets the product requirements

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:28.000
Whereas quality assurance is assuring that the parts that are going in, if you're building a physical product, or if you're building an information product, that the data that you're getting

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:41.000
that it meets its requirements to be sure that when you've assembled that final product, it will meet its requirements. It'll pass data control. So you're trying to assure

00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:48.000
The process is working properly so that quality control succeeds in the end

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:55.000
And then the analysis and improvement is that there's always going to be issues that you find. There will always be problems

00:27:55.000 --> 00:28:01.000
Some that are anticipated, some that are not anticipated, that are surprising

00:28:01.000 --> 00:28:13.000
If you think about physical manufacturing, right, you can have drift in the machines, the machine bearings are wearing down, things are getting out of… and you have measurements that you take. You've got tolerances

00:28:13.000 --> 00:28:31.000
So, you can think in manufacturing that data quality assurance is measuring tolerances of parts. Are the parts meeting the dimensions, the specifications that you need so that they will assemble together and make a final working product that works correctly?

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:46.000
Just think about the thousands of things that you put together in an automobile if you're making a quality automobile. And so quality assurance is measuring all those things, measuring the axle diameters

00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:48.000
The,

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:59.000
things that you're doing on the interior and the knobs, and all the holes in the dashboard are in the right place and all that. Those are assuring that they will all fit together in the end

00:28:59.000 --> 00:29:07.000
In complete, you'll have a car that passes the quality control

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:19.000
for that automobile. It'll start, the interior's clean, you can drive it away, the steering wheel works, all of these things, they go through… I'm sorry, probably hundreds of checks

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:26.000
For each car that they manufacture, to be sure that it meets quality control

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:41.000
The same thing should be happening for information products, but then you're going to find things that don't work out. You're going to find that there are certain parts that don't meet tolerances. You're going to find that even though the parts

00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:48.000
met all the tolerances. In the end, what you're building just doesn't seem to work correctly

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:55.000
And that failure of quality control. So these failures of quality assurance and quality control

00:29:55.000 --> 00:30:10.000
You take and you collect those, and you analyze them and see, you know, was this just a fluke? Somebody had a bad day on the shop floor, or is this something, we're getting something wrong from this particular supplier

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:20.000
We need to correct that so that you need to constantly analyze and improve your quality control and quality assurance failures

00:30:20.000 --> 00:30:29.000
So that you can act on those, and to try to make corrections to continually improve the process that you're… of building this particular product

00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:38.000
So those have been adopted in the ISO 8000 in the data quality management process reference model.

00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:44.000
And so here's… and you'll see this if you look at the actual standard that I put in the resources folder

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:56.000
So again, there's the three overall processes, the implementation process, which has four parts. It's the plan. You can see there's quality planning up on the upper left

00:30:56.000 --> 00:31:00.000
And then that leads to

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:08.000
quality control, which leads to quality assessment or assurance, if you will, should put assurance there

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:23.000
And then… then you do data quality improvement. So it really follows the plan, do, check, act cycle in the four parts. Now there are other things that are related to it. We talked about back in the Principles

00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:31.000
Even though you're getting very good data, if you don't have a good data architecture, you still won't succeed.

00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:43.000
If you don't have the right database schema, if you're not using the right processes to manipulate the data, you need to understand what's the data architecture, how do you move data around, transfer it

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:46.000
One of the things that

00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:54.000
So some of you noticed, not everyone in this last assignment when you were looking at the movie file

00:31:54.000 --> 00:32:00.000
If you… some of you noticed that one of the director's names had a strange character in it

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:07.000
And so this has to do with the data transfer. If you look at the original file

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:14.000
You'll see that it's in there correctly. In the last name, I'm trying to remember the guy's last name

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:20.000
But anyway, it had a Spanish character in it, a grave accented O.

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:24.000
Something like

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:34.000
Calderon or something like that. Anyway, the last part of his last name was R-O-N, but the O was an accented O in Spanish

00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:38.000
The guy was a Spaniard. And so

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:42.000
And if you look at it, it depends on

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:49.000
what system you're using if it comes out correctly. So, for example, if you opened it in Notepad

00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:53.000
or a notepad or

00:32:53.000 --> 00:33:01.000
No plus plus, whatever that is. You'd see it's correct that that character is rendered correctly

00:33:01.000 --> 00:33:07.000
But one of the things, if you're using OpenRefine and you open that file and open refine

00:33:07.000 --> 00:33:12.000
It looks like some kind of Oriental character

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:15.000
It misinterprets that character, doesn't render it properly.

00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:29.000
The underlying data hasn't changed, but it's rendering has changed. And it actually gives you even a different one if you open it in Excel. So this gets back to data architecture and data transfer management

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:36.000
So that you can understand correctly what data you're really looking at.

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:49.000
In their data operations management, how do you overall, how does your process work? And then, of course, a very important one now is data security. It's becoming more and more important to prevent data leakage

00:33:49.000 --> 00:33:53.000
Especially in highly regulated organizations

00:33:53.000 --> 00:34:10.000
And then, finally, there's resource provisioning. You know, how are you managing? What's the structure, the hierarchy of management? Who has roles? What are the roles well defined of who's supposed to be doing what? Do you have a data product manager? Is there somebody

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:24.000
That's bridging this gap between the consumer of the product and the people who are building it, so that you can always be assured that you're building the right thing for the consumer.

00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:32.000
And are people getting training? The systems they're using, people are always changing, using different types of

00:34:32.000 --> 00:34:44.000
Infotechnology and their processes and are the people getting trained for their roles? Do they know how to operate these systems

00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:50.000
And know what the procedures are. This is especially importance now in the area of data governance

00:34:50.000 --> 00:35:01.000
Where you have to follow certain rules about the decisions you make on data. And so you can't just go in and change things or make changes in processes

00:35:01.000 --> 00:35:11.000
unilaterally, you have to go through a process that's defined in the data governance. So, data governance gives the rules

00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:21.000
or how you manage data, some people have said that data management are the decisions that you make about data. What you're going to do with the data

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:31.000
And data governance are the rules for making those decisions. So there's certain rules that are set about of what you can and can't do in your decisions about data.

00:35:31.000 --> 00:35:36.000
And that's data governance. And so that's a very important part of the resource provision

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:42.000
So this is kind of a big overview of the three processes

00:35:42.000 --> 00:35:49.000
The implementation based on the plan, do, check act, then the data related support and the resource provisioning

00:35:49.000 --> 00:35:54.000
So again, talking a little bit here about this

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:59.000
We just talked about the implementation

00:35:59.000 --> 00:36:02.000
process has a four-part

00:36:02.000 --> 00:36:08.000
The planning, the quality control, the quality assurance, and then the analysis and improvement

00:36:08.000 --> 00:36:15.000
So again, looking at it kind of in this analogy of manufacturing

00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:28.000
Then we start right here. We start with planning and requirements. What is it that we're building? What's the design and what is it that the consumer wants? Remember, that was the number one thing in quality management

00:36:28.000 --> 00:36:35.000
What is it that the consumer or the user of this product wants so that we can design that product

00:36:35.000 --> 00:36:39.000
So we designed that product, and once we've designed it

00:36:39.000 --> 00:36:41.000
Then

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:53.000
Again, this is just talking about what you need in that planning and requirements stage. You need written documentation, you need to be clear about who's responsible for meeting the requirements

00:36:53.000 --> 00:36:57.000
And your strategy for improving it

00:36:57.000 --> 00:37:13.000
And then that leads to quality control. You have a plan for building a product that meets consumer needs. So from that, you start building a product, you have requirements for that product based on the consumer's needs

00:37:13.000 --> 00:37:19.000
And you have a design for it. So now you know what the information product is going to look like.

00:37:19.000 --> 00:37:34.000
And that's what I tried to give you in this most recent assignment. I tried to give you a description. It's not necessarily complete at 100%, but it's a description of a particular product that someone wants to build. And from that

00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:45.000
You should be able to infer some of the… what are some of the characteristics you need in the data that goes into it. And so I was asking you to look at one of the sources

00:37:45.000 --> 00:37:52.000
There may be others necessary, but at least one of the sources that you have, which is this

00:37:52.000 --> 00:37:53.000
file of grad

00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:58.000
Because the product we're trying to build is a grade report

00:37:58.000 --> 00:38:10.000
And so based on what we need in the product, how suitable would that file be as an input was one of the raw materials here on the left into that product

00:38:10.000 --> 00:38:15.000
So I'm asking you to start inferring from the product description

00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:22.000
Some of the data requirement descriptions for data coming in

00:38:22.000 --> 00:38:30.000
So we have the planning, we plan a product that's based on consumer user needs, and we have a design for it

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:36.000
Then we say, okay, we're going to build this product, here are the specifications for the product

00:38:36.000 --> 00:38:48.000
And then once we have that, then we can have our quality control. We can say, all right, did we really build the product the way we designed it? So that's the quality control

00:38:48.000 --> 00:38:54.000
And so, again, you have to ask these questions. Do you have that in place? Do you have those written down

00:38:54.000 --> 00:39:08.000
Can you automate that, or is it something that you have to manually do? And if you have to do it manually, can you expect 100% of it? So I had this experience in working with the credit reporting agency

00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:12.000
And they really

00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:16.000
Initially, when I talked to him, this had been several years ago

00:39:16.000 --> 00:39:25.000
They had a really… and you'll find this to be true in most organizations. They had a really good quality assurance process

00:39:25.000 --> 00:39:38.000
When data would come in, when they would receive data, they did a really good job of examining it and being sure it was standardized and complete and timely, all of these good things about the data coming in

00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:47.000
But then they put it into a process to build a credit report. And they didn't really check the credit reports before they sent them out

00:39:47.000 --> 00:40:05.000
What they relied on was that people would get find out about their credit report. The credit reports weren't actually going to the people that the report was about. It was actually going to the companies that were subscribing to credit reports, like banks or furniture stores when you go in to buy something on credit

00:40:05.000 --> 00:40:20.000
What's the first thing they do? They ask you for your name and your social security number, and they put it into the system they subscribe to this reporting agency, and that reporting agency gives back a report that says, well, here's the creditworthiness of this person

00:40:20.000 --> 00:40:22.000
Just trying to buy a sofa from you

00:40:22.000 --> 00:40:38.000
And then, you know, and so the company might deny it, and the person might ask, well, why are you denying it? Or, you know, they may or may not tell them, but that would trigger them to go and write to this credit company and say.

00:40:38.000 --> 00:40:53.000
What's in my credit report, I got turned down. And so really they were really relying on consumers to be their quality control. In other words, they weren't really examining the credit reports they were producing to see if they were good or not

00:40:53.000 --> 00:41:09.000
They were just sending them out based on the fact that they thought they were putting good data into it. Again, it's this thinking that you can reverse the garbage in, garbage out to say, good data in always means that good products will come out

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:11.000
And that's not true

00:41:11.000 --> 00:41:26.000
And so they were subscribed to that theory that they were cleaning the data going in, and therefore, when it went through the process, it would be a good product coming out, and they would just send it out the door without any quality control checks

00:41:26.000 --> 00:41:38.000
And so they started then implementing quality control after the review that we did. And it really improved. Now, one of the reasons I say that is that

00:41:38.000 --> 00:41:47.000
They were producing thousands of these credit reports every day. They were electronic and being sent out to furniture stores and banks and

00:41:47.000 --> 00:41:53.000
automobile dealerships, anywhere people were buying something on credit

00:41:53.000 --> 00:42:00.000
Then the subscribers, these companies were subscribed to the credit reports, were getting these reports

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:13.000
So it was impractical for the company, the credit company, to inspect every one of the credit reports before it was sent out. So they implemented a sampling process

00:42:13.000 --> 00:42:19.000
And so they started and it was

00:42:19.000 --> 00:42:25.000
Very helpful in improving the quality of the credit reports that they were sending out.

00:42:25.000 --> 00:42:34.000
So and again, what they did is what's called stratified sampling. It's not a total random sampling. It's saying

00:42:34.000 --> 00:42:41.000
I'm going to sample based on certain characteristics, and so some of the things that they were

00:42:41.000 --> 00:42:54.000
Looking at was if a credit report had more than one social security number in it got tags for being a possible, there's more likely that there could be errors in it

00:42:54.000 --> 00:42:59.000
Or if somebody had changed addresses more than 2 or 3 times

00:42:59.000 --> 00:43:14.000
Then these were all triggers to say, these are the ones that are higher priority that we should sample from, because these are the ones that, in the past have proved to be more problematic in terms of errors.

00:43:14.000 --> 00:43:18.000
So then that gets us to quality assurance

00:43:18.000 --> 00:43:24.000
That's going back into the process, saying, based on the requirements I have for my information product

00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:26.000
or service

00:43:26.000 --> 00:43:36.000
What do I need to be careful about in terms of the… what are the requirements that I need for the raw materials and the systems or the processes that I'm using to build that product

00:43:36.000 --> 00:43:42.000
And that's basically in a manufacturing that would be measuring tolerances

00:43:42.000 --> 00:43:49.000
In an information product, we measure them in terms of data quality dimensions that we've talked about earlier

00:43:49.000 --> 00:43:56.000
There are many dimensional frameworks, but almost all of them are based around certain things, such as

00:43:56.000 --> 00:44:06.000
The accuracy of the data, the completeness of the data, the consistency of the representation of the data, the timeliness of the data

00:44:06.000 --> 00:44:10.000
There are other dimensions, too, that have been neglected in the past

00:44:10.000 --> 00:44:18.000
The ones that we're talking about here are things like the validity, the consistency of the data. Is everything spell

00:44:18.000 --> 00:44:31.000
You know, if I have 10 options for a value and I know what those options are, what if I get something that's not one of those 10 values? Is it something that I can correct or standardize

00:44:31.000 --> 00:44:41.000
These are all automated type things. I could… validity is one, a big go-to. Another one is completeness, right? I can detect null and like values

00:44:41.000 --> 00:44:52.000
Not quite always as easy to detect placeholder values, but at least non-null and blank values I can detect. So those are automated checks

00:44:52.000 --> 00:45:04.000
But there are some very important ones, especially now in the age of AI that are not as easy to automate and require more work. Things like objectivity. Do I have bias in the data

00:45:04.000 --> 00:45:09.000
What about the reputation of the data? So there are many things that

00:45:09.000 --> 00:45:22.000
The qualities of the data that we need to know about, but they're not easily programmed into a rule-based programming system. They require some other types of investigations, like surveys

00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:25.000
or interviews

00:45:25.000 --> 00:45:42.000
And so this is one of the obstacles in adapting the manufacturing model to information quality. We say there's a nice analogy here. We've got raw materials that are source data. We've got machinery that's programs, we're building products

00:45:42.000 --> 00:45:58.000
But in real manufacturing, the tolerances are really well understood, right? We know how to measure diameter, we know how to measure density or tensile strength, all these kinds of things that are pretty well

00:45:58.000 --> 00:46:10.000
clear to most people and fairly easy to do. But when it comes to data, there's a lot of intangible things are what we call anti-kinesthetic

00:46:10.000 --> 00:46:13.000
They're not something that

00:46:13.000 --> 00:46:19.000
We can touch, right? It's not something we can put on measuring tape or a caliper on to measure

00:46:19.000 --> 00:46:28.000
And so it becomes a little more difficult. And this was a big stumbling block in the early days of information quality

00:46:28.000 --> 00:46:40.000
bringing it into a discipline is how do you measure those tolerances? What are the analogies of measurement tolerances in data? And that's what we developed these data

00:46:40.000 --> 00:46:46.000
quality dimensional frameworks, like the Wang Strong that we mentioned, the 16 dimension

00:46:46.000 --> 00:46:53.000
And again, there are many of those dimensions that people have ignored up until now in the age of AI

00:46:53.000 --> 00:46:58.000
the things like the reputation and the

00:46:58.000 --> 00:47:13.000
the objectivity and the usefulness of the data, the relevance, things like that were not easily put into rules that you could put in an informatic data quality system or an IBM quality system

00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:24.000
They were often ignored, but now they've become very important. And so now we're looking at ways to try to measure all of these dimensions.

00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:33.000
And then, again, if you need, you're going to look at your process. Do you really have those QA processes in place

00:47:33.000 --> 00:47:43.000
And again, the same question, can they all be automated? Or some of them need to be manual? And if they're manual, do you need to sample?

00:47:43.000 --> 00:47:46.000
Is it impossible to

00:47:46.000 --> 00:47:49.000
Manually inspect every one of the products.

00:47:49.000 --> 00:47:56.000
So again, we come back to this and again, I hope you'll remember this

00:47:56.000 --> 00:48:01.000
Quality control and quality assurance have different meanings, and they're not interchangeable

00:48:01.000 --> 00:48:17.000
So try to use them properly. Again, quality control applies to the finished product, whether that's an information product or a manufactured product. Is it meeting the requirements that you set up based on the consumer's needs?

00:48:17.000 --> 00:48:22.000
Quality assurance only applies to the process of building the product

00:48:22.000 --> 00:48:30.000
And the product component and building process meet the requirements to get you to a product that will pass quality control

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:36.000
So finally, the fourth part of the process is we've started with

00:48:36.000 --> 00:48:47.000
What is it we're trying to build? Then once we know what it is, what are going to be the requirements for that product that we can check to be sure we built the right product? That's the quality control

00:48:47.000 --> 00:48:52.000
Then we back into the system for building it, and we have quality assurance

00:48:52.000 --> 00:48:56.000
But then the last thing is when we have failures

00:48:56.000 --> 00:49:04.000
And we will. There will always be some issues. Sometimes they're just inescapable

00:49:04.000 --> 00:49:15.000
things that happen, but we want to analyze and be sure wasn't just a one-time thing? Was it an accident was a fluke? It's probably not going to happen again. Or is there something

00:49:15.000 --> 00:49:30.000
Endemic to this process, or to the data that we're getting, or somewhere in the system, is there something that's causing this to happen over and over again? And if it is, can we go back and address it and fix it

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:32.000
So

00:49:32.000 --> 00:49:38.000
We don't have… we can start eliminating those failures, or at least reducing them

00:49:38.000 --> 00:49:45.000
So that, again, we have less and less effort that we have to spend when we finally get to the product

00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:56.000
Because once we get to the product, and it's not meeting requirements, then we either have to put it back in to fix it, which takes time and energy, or we have to discard that product

00:49:56.000 --> 00:50:02.000
And in which case, we've lost all the effort that we put into building that particular product

00:50:02.000 --> 00:50:05.000
So

00:50:05.000 --> 00:50:10.000
So it's important that we try to get to the best products

00:50:10.000 --> 00:50:16.000
that are… that meet all of the quality control requirements as much as possible

00:50:16.000 --> 00:50:22.000
And our goal would be 100%, but again, we have to keep checking because things always go wrong

00:50:22.000 --> 00:50:28.000
And we need to constantly try to improve our processes. So this is

00:50:28.000 --> 00:50:41.000
Again, you can't do that unless you systematically collect those issues. So another problem I find in many organizations is, yes, they'll find the data quality problem while they're doing the process

00:50:41.000 --> 00:50:43.000
And they'll make a ticket and say, oh.

00:50:43.000 --> 00:50:51.000
Okay, this needs to be fixed. We can't send this out the way it is, and so they'll immediately jump on it and fix it. But then

00:50:51.000 --> 00:51:02.000
They don't really collect in a database all of those tickets so that they can periodically go back, say, at the end of the month and say, let's look at all of our problems that we had, all the tickets that we

00:51:02.000 --> 00:51:14.000
We registered and see if there's some kind of pattern in there that we could, are there some systematic quality control or quality assurance failure that we could improve the process?

00:51:14.000 --> 00:51:34.000
So we can look for the root causes of any kind of persistence. So one thing is trying to sort out, is this a persistent issue? If it's a persistent issue, is it an important one? How often does it occur? And then we'd make a decision, say, okay, we're going to try to address that particular issue

00:51:34.000 --> 00:51:43.000
and fix it and then we continually do that over time so that we try to continually improve our process.

00:51:43.000 --> 00:51:56.000
So that we can implement those processes back into our system and continue to make it better. So those are things that

00:51:56.000 --> 00:52:08.000
We really can't say that we have some kind of a quality process, whether this is information quality or manufacturing quality. If we don't have these things, we have to have

00:52:08.000 --> 00:52:12.000
A plan and quality control requirements

00:52:12.000 --> 00:52:16.000
That should lead to quality assurance requirements

00:52:16.000 --> 00:52:21.000
And then the other thing is that we need to collect those failures

00:52:21.000 --> 00:52:38.000
And always try to find root causes of problems if we can, to continually improve. So if we don't have those things, if we're not, we don't have requirements and we're checking for those requirements, whether they're quality control or quality assurance requirements

00:52:38.000 --> 00:52:46.000
And then we're collecting and we're not collecting failures, we can't say that we have a quality system

00:52:46.000 --> 00:52:53.000
And many quality management systems that I've looked at, especially data quality management

00:52:53.000 --> 00:52:56.000
are lacking one or more of those things

00:52:56.000 --> 00:53:02.000
And so they're really not doing data quality management

00:53:02.000 --> 00:53:07.000
So again, once you find these things, you have to decide what you're going to

00:53:07.000 --> 00:53:13.000
try to fix what's the cost versus the benefit of these improvements

00:53:13.000 --> 00:53:16.000
And then the thing that's often missed is

00:53:16.000 --> 00:53:22.000
communicating when you do fix things and make things better and bragging about it

00:53:22.000 --> 00:53:31.000
So that brings us to the other, the data related support processes that we talked about and that's the data architecture. That's very important

00:53:31.000 --> 00:53:35.000
We may have all the right data that we need to build

00:53:35.000 --> 00:53:49.000
an information product that meets consumers' needs, but if we don't architect the system for building it, for storing the data, representing the data, we don't have the right database schema, we're not using the right

00:53:49.000 --> 00:54:01.000
Programs were not using the right transformations of the data, then it will fail. So understanding the data architecture that we're using is very important. That

00:54:01.000 --> 00:54:04.000
We have to look at the quality of that

00:54:04.000 --> 00:54:15.000
the data transfer I was talking about, but if you move data around, there's always a chance of losing some of it or changing it, just like the character issue that I just pointed out in the

00:54:15.000 --> 00:54:18.000
little example that you did with the movies

00:54:18.000 --> 00:54:25.000
You'll find that there's a problem in transferring data between your desktop system

00:54:25.000 --> 00:54:28.000
I gave it to you in a CSV format

00:54:28.000 --> 00:54:34.000
that if you look at what we call a UTF-8

00:54:34.000 --> 00:54:38.000
coded characters

00:54:38.000 --> 00:54:52.000
And then that's rendered correctly in most of your systems, but for some reason, OpenRefine did not render that. So when I transferred that data into OpenRefine into another system, things changed

00:54:52.000 --> 00:55:00.000
And it looked like it was incorrect, but really in the base data, it was rendered

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:17.000
Or it was represented correctly, it just wasn't rendered correctly in certain other systems. So somebody might see that and flag that as bad data, or… but again, it's a reflector of the actual architectures and systems that you're using

00:55:17.000 --> 00:55:27.000
And then data operations overall, are you efficiently managing the systems, doing things in the right order and being as efficient as possible

00:55:27.000 --> 00:55:30.000
to get your products bill

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:41.000
And then, last but not least is the data security. Do the right people have access? Everybody that needs to use the data needs access to it, but you don't want any more than that

00:55:41.000 --> 00:55:48.000
You don't want the data to be just accessible to anyone, especially if it

00:55:48.000 --> 00:55:59.000
Any type of personally identifiable information, information that's protected through regulation, some type of financial information

00:55:59.000 --> 00:56:16.000
Things like that, you want to be sure you have that balance between people that need to have it can get to it, but people that shouldn't be seen can't get to it. And that's the whole idea of cybersecurity.

00:56:16.000 --> 00:56:22.000
And then finally, the third part is the resource provisioning process

00:56:22.000 --> 00:56:31.000
Where you really have a data quality organization management, where you have defined roles because again, data quality management

00:56:31.000 --> 00:56:34.000
should be a program versus

00:56:34.000 --> 00:56:39.000
A particular process, I'm sorry, a project

00:56:39.000 --> 00:56:54.000
The difference between a project and a program is that in a project, it has a definite start and end time and a set of objectives that you're going to accomplish by the end of it.

00:56:54.000 --> 00:57:02.000
Whereas in a program is an ongoing process that you're running all the time, right? So data quality management

00:57:02.000 --> 00:57:13.000
You will have projects in, in other words, both like we were talking about, you've identified some root cause of a problem in your system through analyzing your failure tickets

00:57:13.000 --> 00:57:31.000
You would launch a project to say, I'm going to fix that particular project. I'm starting now on this time, I plan to be finished in three months with this. Here's what I plan to accomplish. That would be a project. And so I would do that project to improve my overall

00:57:31.000 --> 00:57:47.000
program, but it's a program in the sense that it has management. I have a director for data quality management, I have data stewards that have certain roles. I have defined roles for people that are ongoing. It's not something that we do on

00:57:47.000 --> 00:57:52.000
time-check basis. It's something that we do continually

00:57:52.000 --> 00:58:00.000
And of course, everyone needs to be trained for the role that they have within this organizational management

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:06.000
And again, this is something… I saw this for a while, and I see it less now

00:58:06.000 --> 00:58:11.000
Is people having formally defined data quality

00:58:11.000 --> 00:58:14.000
organizations or programs

00:58:14.000 --> 00:58:27.000
Where they have a director or a manager, however you want to call it, of information or data quality and roles. A lot of this has kind of been absorbed in data governance

00:58:27.000 --> 00:58:32.000
Typically, data quality is one of the parts of data governance

00:58:32.000 --> 00:58:38.000
And so, but oftentimes it's not set aside very specifically

00:58:38.000 --> 00:58:50.000
With a specific set of requirements, like having quality control requirements documented, having quality assurance requirements documented, things like this, having

00:58:50.000 --> 00:58:57.000
A review process, collecting your

00:58:57.000 --> 00:59:03.000
collecting your data here, I'm sorry, your quality control, quality assurance requirements, and so forth

00:59:03.000 --> 00:59:12.000
And so often that kind of gets swept under the rug, but it really needs to be visible whether you're putting it as a separate

00:59:12.000 --> 00:59:24.000
organization, you have a data quality management organization, or if it's umbrellaed under data governance, it still needs to have its own set of standards

00:59:24.000 --> 00:59:27.000
So this

00:59:27.000 --> 00:59:34.000
If you really want to comply with this standard, the ISO 8061 standard that we talked about

00:59:34.000 --> 00:59:38.000
And again, the full documentation I put in the resources

00:59:38.000 --> 00:59:45.000
It's really compliance is you do a self-study, you document what you're doing

00:59:45.000 --> 00:59:48.000
That's identified in the standard

00:59:48.000 --> 01:00:04.000
And so, there's activities that should be performed and outcomes that should be achieved. And these are defined in the standard. So there's 51 activities that you should be undertaking in your data quality management, and you should be obtaining 82 outcomes

01:00:04.000 --> 01:00:08.000
And so part of compliance is to document

01:00:08.000 --> 01:00:21.000
investigating, you know, study, do it like when you would do a self-study of your organization and show what you're doing. Are you doing these 51 activities?

01:00:21.000 --> 01:00:26.000
If you are, or what outcomes are you getting, and so you need to document all those

01:00:26.000 --> 01:00:32.000
And then overall, the extent to which you're improving data quality

01:00:32.000 --> 01:00:35.000
And then back to this support idea

01:00:35.000 --> 01:00:41.000
Do you really have assigned roles and responsibility for data management across the organization?

01:00:41.000 --> 01:00:56.000
And what are the resources that you have available for performing data quality management? And then the one that really gets you is how data quality management is applied anywhere the organization is creating or using data

01:00:56.000 --> 01:00:59.000
So this is a big one

01:00:59.000 --> 01:01:14.000
You may think, well, this is a bank and I'm producing bank statements, but there may be an administrator or secretary up in the C-suite working for the CEO or the CMO

01:01:14.000 --> 01:01:21.000
And they're putting together a spreadsheet for somebody to use. That qualifies here

01:01:21.000 --> 01:01:33.000
If you're building a spreadsheet, you're creating or using data. Are you applying these data management principles? And does that come under your study that you're documenting?

01:01:33.000 --> 01:01:35.000
So this is

01:01:35.000 --> 01:01:39.000
One of the requirements to comply

01:01:39.000 --> 01:01:43.000
So that brings us to assessing and again

01:01:43.000 --> 01:01:44.000
As I just

01:01:44.000 --> 01:01:48.000
or pointed out

01:01:48.000 --> 01:01:53.000
It doesn't make sense to talk about data quality assessment

01:01:53.000 --> 01:01:57.000
If you don't have data quality requirements

01:01:57.000 --> 01:02:05.000
And sometimes you may have these, you may know what they are if you really documented

01:02:05.000 --> 01:02:12.000
But oftentimes you have to develop those yourself.

01:02:12.000 --> 01:02:18.000
So this leads to what I call the data quality assessment triangle

01:02:18.000 --> 01:02:20.000
You have data quality requirements

01:02:20.000 --> 01:02:30.000
That leads you to profiling or analysis data like you did in this assignment. I asked you to do

01:02:30.000 --> 01:02:35.000
An analysis of the movie file, but I didn't give you any documented data requirements

01:02:35.000 --> 01:02:42.000
So all you were doing is just this third part down here, data profiling statistics

01:02:42.000 --> 01:02:46.000
Just looking at the data and seeing what's in there

01:02:46.000 --> 01:02:51.000
without having the data requirements

01:02:51.000 --> 01:03:00.000
You can't really assess the quality. And again, since I didn't give you any process, you're not looking at a process to build the product

01:03:00.000 --> 01:03:03.000
You can't really look at the data architecture

01:03:03.000 --> 01:03:11.000
So, to do a full data quality assessment, you really need all three of these things. You need documented data requirements

01:03:11.000 --> 01:03:24.000
You need to look at the data to see what's in there, but you also need to look at the architecture and the process that's being used to build the product

01:03:24.000 --> 01:03:28.000
So that's what we call data quality assessment

01:03:28.000 --> 01:03:33.000
To add to it the information quality

01:03:33.000 --> 01:03:49.000
assessment. You really need to understand how the business is using these products and what is the value they're producing. Because remember, our definition of information quality is maximizing the value and minimizing the risk

01:03:49.000 --> 01:03:53.000
of the information that you have in your organization

01:03:53.000 --> 01:04:05.000
Right, and also for the information products that you're building, are you creating value for the users? So the IQ assessment is based on

01:04:05.000 --> 01:04:07.000
the value proposition

01:04:07.000 --> 01:04:15.000
And that should be directly related to the data quality, which is just being sure that you're building things correctly.

01:04:15.000 --> 01:04:21.000
It doesn't answer the question, am I building the right thing? Am I building what the consumer wants?

01:04:21.000 --> 01:04:34.000
If I'm building what the consumer wants, then I can back into the data quality and say, all right, so what do I need to build that quality product for that consumer

01:04:34.000 --> 01:04:39.000
So that becomes the information quality. I have to add on the value proposition

01:04:39.000 --> 01:04:48.000
I can be very busy building products, but if they're products that nobody likes or uses or complains about.

01:04:48.000 --> 01:05:01.000
They're not quality. And so I might have good data quality. I might have a well-documented process for building something, but if I'm building something that's not working properly

01:05:01.000 --> 01:05:10.000
are not satisfying building, I'm sorry, creating value for the user that I don't have information quality

01:05:10.000 --> 01:05:12.000
Only data quality

01:05:12.000 --> 01:05:17.000
So, what we're trying to do is close this gap

01:05:17.000 --> 01:05:21.000
And say, don't just consider the data

01:05:21.000 --> 01:05:26.000
We have to look at it from the standpoint of the product

01:05:26.000 --> 01:05:29.000
We have to manage information as a product

01:05:29.000 --> 01:05:36.000
We're building some kind of information product or service for some use by consumer

01:05:36.000 --> 01:05:40.000
Then that should be the trigger that starts all of this.

01:05:40.000 --> 01:05:47.000
We can get lost in the details and that can become a problem for us

01:05:47.000 --> 01:05:53.000
